Under specific conditions on the nonlinearity $f$, we demonstrate that if the perturbation parameter $\mu$ is sufficiently small, there exists a solution within a prescribed bounded region. Additionally, we establish the existence of ground state solutions for a coupled system of Schrödinger-Perez equations.

\begin{cases}
-\Delta u + Q(y)u^p = \lambda u & \text{in } \mathbb{R}^2, \\
u > 0, \quad u \in C^{2,\alpha}(\mathbb{R}^2) \cap C_0(\mathbb{R}^2).
\end{cases}
$$
Here $C_0(\mathbb{R}^2)$ denotes the space of continuous functions vanishing at infinity. The parameter $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$, the exponent $p>1$ is supercritical, and the potential $Q(y)$ is a bounded function. By applying perturbation techniques to a specific class of potentials $Q(y)$, we demonstrate the existence of solutions exhibiting degenerate behavior at infinity.
